      "content": "Apologies for the confusion. Let's get straight to the point with your example. In the table you provided, the transitive dependency exists between CustomerID and CustomerName, and CustomerID and CustomerEmail. To move this table from 2NF to 3NF, you would need to eliminate this transitive dependency by creating a new table for the Customer information.\n\nHere's how you can do it:\n\n1. Create a new table called \"Customers\" with the following columns: [CustomerID, CustomerName, CustomerEmail].\n2. Move the CustomerName and CustomerEmail columns from the original table to the new \"Customers\" table.\n3. In the original table, replace the CustomerName and CustomerEmail columns with a single column called CustomerID, which will serve as a foreign key referencing the CustomerID in the \"Customers\" table.\n\nAfter these steps, your original table will look like this:\n\n| OrderID | CustomerID |\n|---------|------------|\n\nAnd the new \"Customers\" table will look like this:\n\n| CustomerID | CustomerName | CustomerEmail |\n|------------|--------------|---------------|\n\nNow, your data is in 3NF, as you've eliminated the transitive dependency by separating the Customer information into its own table. This way",
